WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
 Okay, with Councilman Matt Zall to make your way up. You know Matt Zall, who

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.590
 was first elected to City Council in 2001, representing World of 15, who was

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:11.500
 the group before the short way.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.660
 That's where he and his generation of the standard grow up. He's a strong

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.670
 advocate for the arts, green building, alternative energy. Look what he's done

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:21.500
 with Gordon Square.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.330
 He's chair of the Council Safety Committee. Last November, Matt got a national

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:30.500
 honor. He was elected president of the National League of Cities.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.550
 And of course, he's making your balance now, he's responsible for the annual

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.500
 Italian Heritage Month celebration for City Hall.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.500
 And Councilman Zall is going to induct Jim Craciun.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.780
 On behalf of the City of Cleveland, I can't think of two better ambassadors for

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.710
 what makes Cleveland wonderful, the beautiful mosaic of people who call it home,

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:00.500
 then Bill and then Dan and Debbie Hanson.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.500
 You two do a terrific job.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.510
 Father Guarval, thank you for the beautiful invocation. So I have to reciprocate

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.500
 Chevronifac.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.500
 It means how are you, and the response would be good.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.500
 La Bulsana. Many years.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.500
 So Father Guarval, thank you for the beautiful blessing.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.850
 I do want to honor and recognize the former colleague of mine who I've worked

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:33.030
 so much on in the international community, my former colleague, who's the

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.500
 president, CEO of Cleveland Josephine.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.530
 And so it's my distinct honor and privilege to recognize somebody who has been

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:51.500
 a mentor for me since I was probably a common Roman Catholic.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.570
 So following into the footsteps of his father to care for those in our

00:01:55.570 --> 00:02:00.970
 community who are grieving, I can't think of anyone better and kinder and gentler

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:03.500
 than somebody like Jimmy Craciun.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:10.650
 It's such an admirable thing to do the following footsteps of your parents. And

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.500
 Jim Craciun is a dedicated father of five.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.790
 His kids are here in the well, Jason, Jonathan, Jesse, and Nicholas. And he was

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.500
 a loving and caring husband to the late mercy.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.860
 You know, Jim has always been a very deeply faithful person. He's been a

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.500
 lifelong parishioner relating on carnival.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:35.090
 He's so involved in every aspect of what that church does. And his presence is

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.500
 always known.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:43.870
 And in 1974, Jim's odyssey really began to unfold in his deeper appreciation

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.500
 and love for his capital place, faith.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.840
 You know, risking his own life. Many people don't notice about Jim, but it's in

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:53.500
 the book.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:03:00.480
 Jim smuggled violence and catechisms into Communist Romania. In 1974, he was a

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.500
 young kid.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.500
 This is where he began his lifelong commitment to human rights.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:12.500
 And at the International Human Rights Conference in London in April of 1977,

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:18.140
 Jim helped draft and signed a memorandum condemning the impact of the Helsinki

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.500
 Accords and its lives on the Soviet Bloc countries.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.720
 You know, this same memorandum was prepared and signed simultaneously by

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.060
 Romanian and Czechoslovakians by courageous dissidents who were later

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.500
 imprisoned for this act.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.080
 He risked his life because he believed in the cause. All of us could only

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:44.910
 follow our cause and say that I would put my own life before this issue just to

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.500
 raise awareness.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.940
 So shortly thereafter, I began working at Croatia's uniform. And it was a great

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:56.570
 job. I was a doorman. I learned how to say hello in about 15 different

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:57.500
 languages.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:04.500
 And not only a doorman, I started carving parts. Jim came up to me and he goes,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.500
 "You're only 15. You don't have a job this life."

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.420
 He's a regular people's guy. Like, "You don't have a job this life." But, you

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.500
 know, Jim has always been like a big brother to me.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.340
 And he realized early on, with the recent passing of my father when I started

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.040
 working there, that there wasn't a better way that could impact my life than

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.500
 with giving me a job.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:33.540
 And it wasn't just a job. Jim took an active interest in my education and my

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.500
 well-being. Jim got me involved in the Cleveland JC's.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.500
 He got me involved very active in the Holy Name Society of Our Lady Moncarmo.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.500
 In fact, I encourage you to run for office of that wonderful organization.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:52.550
 And Jim saw something in myself that I wasn't even aware of. He's been telling

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:54.500
 me this for over 40 years.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:05:00.020
 He always said one day that I would one day rise to serve others. And I wasn't

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.500
 quite sure at the time you know you're 15 years old what that phrase meant.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:07.500
 But he had a last-minute, still has a last-minute impact on my life.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.990
 So what I would first consider when running for city council, one of my first

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.500
 meetings, of course, other than Basil Russo, was with Sue Krasner.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:24.500
 In fact, Jim served as my campaign chairman and he opened up many doors for me.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.500
 He still serves as my campaign chairman today.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:30.480
 I know, Jill, I have so much respect for you. And in the two minutes allotted

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.860
 to me tonight, it's not adequate enough time for me to share with this

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:40.500
 wonderful group tonight how much I love and respect you.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.040
 It's my honor and privilege to present for inductee into the Cleveland

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.500
 International Hall of Fame, James Krasner.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:11.500
 So I feel like I'm giving an Academy Award. I want to thank the Academy. I

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:21.500
 actually have the former inductees for voting, all of us unanimously.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:21.500
 To our guests, thank you for being here. I have been blessed with tremendous

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:30.500
 mentors, just as Matt Hilton said that I've been a mentor of his.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:30.500
 I have been blessed with tremendous mentors. And I'd like to share with you a

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:34.500
 few of them.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:39.090
 In fact, when you think about who my mentors were, according to Tom Brokaw,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.500
 they were the greatest generation.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:45.740
 My parents and all the people that were my mentors. I would begin with my

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:50.500
 parents. We know that our parents are our first teachers in the home.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:54.630
 My dad had two Purple Hearts in World War II. My mother loved to embrace two

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:57.500
 kids. She needs a Purple Heart too.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:04.650
 The other person who played a big part of my life was my parish priest. Being a

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:07.500
 young boy at Lady Mark Arnold Church, Father Reno from Scotty,

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:13.410
 who was the Renaissance priest, the visionary of our neighborhood, who began

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.500
 the Detroit Shore Way.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:20.280
 And we're so lucky to have someone like Matt Zone continuing that effort to

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.050
 take Detroit Shore's look down on the whole country as a model for how a city

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.500
 should be in the neighborhoods.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:34.820
 That priest that was my mentor was the young seminary in the Navy before World

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:35.500
 War II.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:41.500
 And he fought the ugly face of Fai Shih-Sih by Benito Mussolini.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:46.500
 When the war was over, the Communists thought that they would take over Italy.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:49.500
 And he fought the ugly face of communism.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:54.510
 And the communist infinity in Rome had a tree where my priest would hang

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.500
 because of his fighting against them.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:00.970
 Of course we know the communist government never did take over, and we were

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.500
 blessed to give Father Reno.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:07.500
 He's the man who asked me to smuggle violence into communist Romania.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:11.500
 And it should have been a long my life with all the work I did for human rights.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.500
 And I am privileged to have met such a priest.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:19.610
 Now I have to talk about another parish priest. Father Vasile Hanigan, the

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:23.500
 Romanian Orthodox priest, I'm half Italian and half Romanian.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:28.010
 Father Vasile Hanigan, he had a church school when my brothers and I and my

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:29.500
 sisters were young.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.880
 We had to go to Romanian school on Saturday. We had to go to the dance in

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.500
 Romania, sing in Romanian,

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:40.500
 speak in Romanian, and pray in Romanian.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:44.500
 And we didn't want to go to the Saturday services. But I'll tell you what,

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:48.500
 today we're so happy that we had Father Vasile Hanigan.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.200
 He was also on the board of the International Services Center, which I have

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.500
 served for many years.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.500
 And he pushed me to be on that board. So I bless his memory.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:02.850
 We have a pillar. You know, it's one thing to have something to say in your

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:03.500
 community.

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:07.500
 It's another thing to have somebody that will publish it in the plain view.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.500
 When the other man was our man, and we bless his memory.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:17.700
 Also we had in our community Peter Lukacs, who was the president of the, all

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.500
 these people I get deceased.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.500
 So if I miss somebody, nobody can yell at me. They probably have one.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:29.930
 But anyways, Peter Lukacs was the publisher of the Romanian newspaper, the Olds

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.500
 in America, called the America.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.760
 Cleveland is the oldest community, has the oldest community of Romanians in

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:37.500
 America.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.680
 The first Romanian Orthodox Church, St. Mary's Romanian Orthodox Church, the

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.500
 first Catholic Church, St. Amminas, and 65th,

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.500
 both founded in the Detroit-Tillway neighborhood.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.500
 So we found out. Thank you.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:58.500
 So Peter Lukacs ran a paper that went all over the world.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:03.500
 And when we talked about what was going on in Romania during the horrible times

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:06.500
 behind the Art Group, the Romanian people living under communism.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:11.880
 Mr. Lukacs gave me a platform. So I'm grateful to this very day and bless his

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:12.500
 memory.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.500
 Next I'd like to remember some of the people in Cleveland that were my life.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.500
 My godfather, godmother. I'd have to say George Dobri.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:23.500
 Who made it, you know, George Dobri, into the Romanian community.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:26.500
 Beautiful man. Fought for our culture.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:31.500
 Another person, who some of you may not know, I remember Stella Zenoni.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:34.500
 What a princess in our Italian community.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:39.500
 And we have wonderful Italians now carrying that towards us, like Alberto Fidelity,

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:42.500
 our basil Russo, Matt Zone.

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:46.970
 Just a beautiful continuation of this beautiful Italian culture, which I'm

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.500
 proud that my mother is out there.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:52.560
 I also want to remember some of those people that were on the board with me, I

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:53.500
 get deceased.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:59.500
 Joe Holzer, who is the German Dornish Robin, who is who has worked, built out

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.500
 the beautiful

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:04.500
 Linnaud Pard on Columbia Road for the German people.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.500
 Joe Holzer was on our board, worked so hard. Bless his memory.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:12.500
 There's somebody here that's living out there. I read more.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.500
 I read more. Was on the board. She pushed me to be chairman of the board.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.500
 For the eight years I was the chairman, I read more.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:24.500
 It was towards me, every step of the way. I owe a lot to Irene Morrell.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:26.500
 Thank you, Irene Morrell.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.500
 I'd like to recognize, I'd like to recognize my brother Joe, who has been my

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:33.500
 partner,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.720
 supported me through the years that I did, went to Paris, went to London for

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:38.500
 human rights,

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.500
 and also was on the board for so many boards, actually.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.500
 Joe was always there covering me at the funeral.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:50.500
 I want to honor my brother Jack, who spent 13 years in China, and has worked

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:50.500
 with the

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.500
 Chinese people and continues his work with China.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:58.500
 So I respect my brother Jack, and now three people were important to me,

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.500
 and one of them is Bishop Anthony Pillow.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:05.500
 I was privileged to be on a first-name basis with Bishop Anthony Pillow,

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:08.500
 and the honor of being the president of all the elite aid societies,

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.500
 and the Catholic Diocese of Cleveland.

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:15.500
 I am so blessed to have been a friend to Bishop Anthony Pillow.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:20.500
 I mean, here, I'm lucky we're the one million Catholics in this Diocese to have

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:22.500
 such a shepherd.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.500
 Another person I'd like to thank is...

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.500
 (applause)

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.500
 Another person I'd like to recognize is Alex Maciaski.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:34.500
 I'm sorry he's not here, but he's been a tremendous mentor of mine,

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:38.500
 and someone who made a young man feel like he was important.

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:43.500
 I'd like you to read where we had that crazy Serbia that's published with Blake

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:43.500
 Dieran,

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.500
 who plays the... you know, the...

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.500
 (inaudible)

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:49.500
 And it's just one of us.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.500
 He cares about all of our nationalities.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:57.500
 And I see my great friend Abby Minna, Abdallah Minna, who is out there for you.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:58.500
 (applause)

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:02.500
 Abdallah Minna, my hero, who straightened out all my understanding,

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.500
 the standings of the Middle East.

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.500
 Thank you, Abdallah. I love you.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:12.500
 And the next person I'd like to recognize is Sam Miller.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:17.500
 For 30 years, for all the organizations I had wanted to belong to and supported

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:24.500
 and needed help, Sam Miller, the humanitarian, the philanthropist at this time,

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.500
 he never refused me.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:27.500
 Never.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:30.500
 And you know what takes money to make this world operate?

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:34.500
 I bless Sam, and I hope God grants him some good help.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:38.500
 Sam was a tremendous man over the last how many years here in Cleveland.

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:41.970
 And now I'd like to tell you a little story about the International Services

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:42.500
 Center.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.500
 We've reached out to refugees, and we're still doing it,

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:47.500
 although I'm not sure what the board is for.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:51.500
 When the Kosovo War happened, we had some refugees from Albania.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:54.500
 There's a husband and a wife, two small children.

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.500
 They came to Cleveland, and we got the husband with, in two days,

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:01.500
 his job in the factory making $7.

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:04.500
 We got the two children in public school.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.500
 We got them a house to live in, to pay rent.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:11.500
 We set up to pay rent for two months to help get on their feet.

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:16.500
 After about a month and a half in Cleveland, United States,

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.500
 I went to our offices.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.500
 We teach them English as a such a language.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:25.500
 So I ran into this obeying fellow, and I said, "How are you doing?"

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:28.500
 And his English wasn't too bad, broken, of course.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.500
 He said, "I am doing wonderful.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:34.500
 I am now making $14 an hour."

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:39.500
 I said, "Oh, my God, $14 an hour translates to $700 a week."

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.500
 I said, "This is unbelievable. How did you do it?"

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:45.500
 He goes, "The place where I work, higher than my wife."

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:52.500
 John F. Kennedy said, and this is important at a time which the Syrian people

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:55.500
 and some of the people are looking to America to help,

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:58.500
 "John F. Kennedy, our great president said,

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:03.500
 'The greatest asset that America has is its immigrants,

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:06.500
 because they appreciate this country.'"

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:13.500
 Now, the folks that like to say this, it has been written,

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:17.500
 "For those that much has been given, much will be asked."

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:22.500
 So if you can be a philanthropist like Sam Miller, continue to be charitable,

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:27.500
 because our charities and our organizations, to make this town a great town,

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:28.500
 they need your help.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:31.500
 If you're a young person and you're thinking about, you know,

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.500
 you don't have a lot of money, but you've been given talents,

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:35.500
 we need your talents.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:38.500
 We need your help. We need your support. We need your time.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:43.500
 Join some good charities and offer your time and talent.

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:47.500
 The last thing I'd like to say, which I've based my whole life on,

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:53.500
 learning from my parents, is that it isn't given that we receive.

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:54.500
 Thank you.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:15:59.140
 [APPLAUSE]

